The evidence is now concrete: at `if_ex_pc=0xf8`, the core latched `if_ex_instr=0x0006e033`, which is the old word around `0xf4`, while the testbench memory/disassembly at `0xf8` is `0x0021f763`. This is a real DUT redirect/refetch timing bug with synchronous instruction memory: after `pc_redirect`, the PC changes one cycle before `i_mem_rdata` reflects the redirected address. I’m adding a one-cycle redirect refetch bubble so IF/EX does not pair the new PC with stale instruction data.
